fw_title:"Times when voluntary prayer is avoided",
fw_b1:"Authentic hadith name times when the Prophet ﷺ forbade prayer: while the sun is rising until it is up, when it is at its height until it passes the meridian, and while it is setting (Sahih Muslim 831); and after the Fajr and Asr prayers until sunrise and sunset (Sahih al-Bukhari 586).",
fw_b2:"Scope: this concerns voluntary (nafl) prayer. It does not tell you to delay or leave an obligatory prayer. Schools and scholars differ on exceptions, for example making up missed obligatory prayers or prayers with a reason such as greeting the mosque, and on how long each period lasts. Ask a qualified scholar if unsure.",
fw_b3:"Method: sunrise, solar noon (the sun crossing the meridian, which is when Dhuhr begins in this app) and sunset are calculated for your location and date with the same solar formulas as the prayer times, never a fixed clock time such as 12:00. The sources give no minutes; this app uses about 15 minutes after sunrise, 10 minutes before Dhuhr and 15 minutes before sunset as approximations. The after-Fajr and after-Asr notes appear only when that prayer is ticked in today's tracker.",
fw_s1:"Sahih Muslim 831 ('Uqbah ibn 'Amir: the three times)",fw_s2:"Sahih al-Bukhari 586 (Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: after Fajr and Asr)",fw_s3:"Ibn Baz, Awqat an-nahy 'an salat an-nafilah (fatwa, Arabic): five times and the exceptions he held",
fw_checked:"References checked on sunnah.com and binbaz.org.sa on 19 October 2026. Not reviewed by a qualified scholar for this app.",
zk_disclaimer:"This calculator provides a general educational estimate, not a binding religious ruling or financial advice. Zakat obligations and eligible deductions may differ according to the type of assets, circumstances and scholarly interpretation. Verify current Nisab values and consult a qualified scholar or trusted Zakat authority if you are unsure.",
zk_none:"No assets entered yet.",zk_invalid:"Some entries were not valid numbers, were negative or were unrealistically large, so they are counted as 0: {f}.",zk_negnet:"Your debts are larger than your assets, so the net is 0.",zk_break:"How this was calculated",zk_total:"Total zakatable assets",
zk_formula:"Formula: net = total assets − debts due now. If net ≥ nisab (grams × price per gram), Zakat = 2.5% × net. Each amount is counted once.",
zk_s1:"1. Nisab and prices",zk_s2:"2. Zakatable assets",zk_s2h:"Enter today's value of what you own, each item once.",zk_s3:"3. Deductions",zk_s3h:"Only debts due now (or soon) are deducted here. Rules for long-term debts and mortgages differ.",
zk_weights:"Nisab weights",zk_w_tola:"87.48 g gold / 612.36 g silver",zk_w_g85:"85 g gold / 595 g silver",
zh_cur:"Every amount below is in this currency.",zh_price:"No price is built in. Use today's price per gram from a dealer or bank, in the currency above.",zh_cash:"Cash at home and money in bank accounts.",zh_gold:"Value of gold you own, including jewellery (scholars differ on jewellery worn regularly).",zh_silver:"Value of silver you own.",zh_inv:"Market value of shares and funds (some scholars count only the zakatable part).",zh_biz:"Value of goods held for sale.",zh_recv:"Money others owe you that you expect to be repaid.",zh_debt:"Debts and bills due now or soon, not the whole of a long-term loan.",
zk_learn:"Learn about Zakat",zk_refs:"References",
zq_what:"What is Zakat?",za_what:"Zakat is an obligatory yearly payment of a share of certain kinds of wealth, given to the groups of people the Qur'an names (9:60), such as the poor and those in need. It is one of the five pillars of Islam.",
zq_nisab:"What is the nisab?",za_nisab:"The nisab is the minimum wealth a person must hold before Zakat is due. It is measured by the value of a set weight of gold or silver, so it changes with metal prices. This calculator multiplies the chosen weight by the price per gram you enter.",
zq_hawl:"The lunar year (hawl)",za_hawl:"Zakat on money, gold, silver and trade goods is due once wealth at or above the nisab has been held for one lunar (Hijri) year, about 354 days. The calculator cannot track this for you; note the date your wealth first reached the nisab.",
zq_est:"What does this calculator count?",za_est:"It adds what you own that is usually zakatable (cash, gold and silver, shares and investments, business stock, money owed to you that you expect back) and subtracts debts due now. If what is left is at least the nisab, the estimate is 2.5% of it.",
zq_metal:"Why do gold and silver give different results?",za_metal:"Silver is much cheaper per gram, so the silver nisab is far lower than the gold nisab and more people reach it. Many organisations recommend silver for that reason; some scholars prefer gold. Choose the basis you follow; the breakdown shows which one was used.",
zq_differ:"Why can rulings differ?",za_differ:"Scholars differ on details such as jewellery worn regularly, shares (whole value or only the zakatable part), business assets, long-term debts and mortgages, money owed to you, and retirement savings such as superannuation that you cannot yet access. Ask a qualified scholar or a trusted Zakat authority about your situation.",
zr_b8:"Hadith evidence: \u201cIslam is built on five\u201d (Ibn 'Umar), which names giving Zakat among the pillars of Islam. It shows that Zakat is obligatory; it does not settle detailed calculation questions.",zr_b8s:"Sahih al-Bukhari 8 (sunnah.com)",
zr_ira:"Explanatory guidance from a charity (not hadith): Islamic Relief Australia explains nisab weights (85 g gold and 595 g silver, and other conventions), the lunar year, which assets count, which debts it deducts, and superannuation, and publishes current nisab values.",zr_iras:"Islamic Relief Australia: Zakat calculator",
zk_checked:"References checked on 19 October 2026. This page has not been reviewed by a qualified scholar.",
